Subject: Let edge keyframe markers overflow their lane so the whole marker can be hit

Freecut MIT adaptation (FL-100). The linked Edit keyframe lanes have no edge
inset: the key of the first or last visible frame is centred on the lane
edge. Each lane clipped its content, so only 5 of that marker's 12 pixels
were drawn and reachable, and its centre belonged to the cell border (left)
or the scrollbar strip (right). An ordinary click on it hit a neighbour.

Markers of a lane without an edge inset now live in their own layer, clipped
half a marker outside the axis instead of at the lane. A marker on either
edge is drawn and hit whole, over the property column on the left and the
scrollbar strip on the right, and stays on its frame. Everything else in the
cell (grid lines, blocked ranges, bands, connectors, easing spans) is still
clipped by the lane, and a key panned off the axis still disappears. The
overflowing layer takes no pointer input itself; only a marker's own box
does, so the property column's controls and the scrollbar keep every pixel
outside it.

A scroll container clips at its padding edge, so in that presentation the
sheet viewport now spans the scrollbar strip as padding instead of stopping
before it. The rows keep their width, and the measured sheet width subtracts
the strip. Lanes with an edge inset (the standalone and Motion sheets) keep
the lane as their marker clip and are laid out as before.

